 ],
 "notes": "There are 40 stations, Nos. 81–120. The list sits under the heading 'West Coast of Scotland.' (line 1825). The coast field gives the sub-heading each row falls under: Mainland (81–82), Outer Hebrides (83–88), and Mainland, Skye, and Inner Hebrides (89–120).\n\nMARKS: These are the return asterisks, copied with their spacing because the spacing shows which year each one belongs to. The columns are headed \"1879, '80, '81.\" (line 1829), with stars at text columns 5, 10 and 16.\n- Three stars (all three years): 81, 82, 83, 85, 86, 91, 93, 95, 96, 100, 102, 103, 111, 112, 113, 114, 116.\n- '80 and '81 only (\"*     *\"): 84, 87, 90, 98, 101, 110, 117, 119.\n- 1879 and '80 only (\"*    *\"): 92, 99, 104.\n- '81 only (single star): 109, 115.\n- No stars: 88, 89, 94, 97, 105–108, 118, 120.\nThat gives 27 stars for 1881 against the 26 returns the text gives. One possible reason: 93 and 94 share a brace, so the Hynish/Skerryvore pair may have sent one return, or one of the stars may be a misprint.\n\nOTHER: The numbers are heights. The first row prints \"400 ft.\" and the rows below it use ditto marks ('195  \"' means 195 ft.). The list switches back to \"180 ft.\" at 92 and then uses dittos again. No 'character' column is printed.\n\nBRACKETS:\n- 93/94: One brace joins \"Hynish Signal Tower, Tiree\" and \"Skerryvore, off Tiree\". The stars, \"150 ft.\" and \"W. Crow.\" are printed only on 93's line, but they probably apply to the whole bracket. 150 ft. matches Skerryvore's light, and Hynish was its shore signal station. I put W. Crow and the height under 93 only, as printed, and gave 94 the marks \"{\". Decide whether to attach W. Crow and the height to 94 too.\n- 86 Monach Island: The brace here groups two heights, \"{ 150 \" }\" and \"{ 62 \" }\", which may be two lights. It does not group stations.\n- 83 Butt of Lewis: The brace groups two keepers. I split \"G. Edgar and / Alex Thompson.\" into two entries and dropped the joining \"and\". I kept the trailing full stops on all keeper names, as printed.\n\nADDITIONS: For 119 and 120 the \"1880,\" before the name is the year the station was added (per the lead-in \"Stations added have the dates preceding the names\"), so I put it in other. For 119, \"Isle of / Man (L.V.)\" is split across two lines. For 120, \"Langness do.\" uses \"do.\" (ditto), probably repeating \"Isle of Man\". It is unclear whether it also repeats \"(L.V.)\": Langness was a lighthouse, not a light-vessel. I left place as \"do.\" and type null.\n\nSPELLINGS COPIED AS PRINTED:\n- \"Island Glass\" = Eilean Glas, Scalpay.\n- \"Rhu Stoir\" = Stoer Head.\n- \"Bona, Skye\" is probably a misprint for Rona (Rona light, off Skye, about 220 ft.). There is a Bona light, but it is on Loch Ness.\n- \"Conan Ferry, Loch Eil\" is probably Corran Ferry.\n- \"Dhuheartach\" = Dubh Artach.\n- \"Lochindaul\" = Loch Indaal.\n- \"Chickens Rock\" = Chicken Rock.\n- \"Lamlash, Arran\" is presumably the Holy Isle light.\n- \"Ardnamurchan, Pt. Argyle\": I took \"Ardnamurchan, Pt.\" as the name (Point) and \"Argyle\" as the place. There is no comma after \"Pt.\".\n- \"Kyleakin, Rosshire\": the place is printed as Rosshire.\n\nKEEPERS TO CHECK AGAINST OTHER YEARS: \"J. Youngclause.\" and \"W. Maill.\" are odd-looking names. There are two Ewings at different stations: J. Ewing at Dhuheartach and John Ewing at Skervuile.",
